Task Description:
The overwhelming majority of practical combustion processes involve non-premixed combustion of hydrocarbons. These processes must be efficient and the resulting emissions low. For example, soot production, which can adversely affect efficiency, emissions, and equipment lifetime, must be controlled, and flame extinction must be avoided. Many methods have been considered to reduce soot, such as increasing strain rates and inert concentrations and reducing pressures, but these techniques generally weaken the flame, leading to extinction and resulting in substantial performance penalties. The proposed study considers a novel method of approaching combustion to address these concerns. The approach is termed 'Flame Design' as it attempts to take our fundamental understanding of diffusion flames and, within the inherent constraints imposed on the problem, design flames that best optimize efficiency and minimize pollutants. Recognizing the unique benefits of spherical diffusion flames, employ such flames to:
1. Obtain soot-inception limits as functions of stoichiometric mixture fraction, flow direction (direction across the flame front, i.e., towards the fuel or towards the oxidizer), amount of inert (nitrogen) and pressure for ethylene and methane flames. Identify the corresponding soot-inception temperature;
2. Determine effects of stoichiometric mixture fraction and flow direction on the phenomenon of permanently blue flames and, to this end, determine the effects of stoichiometric mixture fraction and flow direction on flame temperature, size, color and soot volume fraction;
3. Obtain extinction limits as functions of stoichiometric mixture fraction, flow direction, amount of inert and pressure for ethylene and methane flames. Identify the corresponding extinction temperature;
4. Evaluate whether this flame system can be used to obtain a pseudo-flammability limit for non-premixed flames in terms of stoichiometric mixture fraction and amount of inert. If so, determine these limits for ethylene and methane flames. Soot formation and flame extinction are problems of great concern in practical combustors. Recent normal-gravity experiments using counterflow diffusion flames have shown that permanently-blue flames (which are devoid of soot regardless of strain rate) and extinction-resistant flames can be produced for many hydrocarbon fuels by nitrogen exchange (increased stoichiometric mixture fraction). Furthermore, there is a unique stoichiometric mixture fraction wherein the flame temperature at extinction can be up to 200 K lower and the strain rate up to four times greater than that for the standard hydrocarbon/air flame with the same flame temperature. The present experiments employ microgravity conditions to allow for a unique set of experiments to clarify the underlying mechanisms responsible for flame response to stoichiometric mixture fraction.

Task Progress:
A tremendous amount of model work was conducted and experimental data obtained in KC-135 A flights at microgravity and reduced gravity were analyzed and compared to predictions. Four technical journal papers were written (two appearred in print and two are under review) based on the work conducted. Refer to the Bibliography section below for additional details.
The topics covered during this period include:
1. the observation of multiple cool flames at mg and the development of a model that includes diffusive transport of heat and species as well as natural convection that proved able to capture the qualitative behavior; the results suggest that enhanced conduction heat transfer can provide the required heat loss to sustain cool flame oscillations;
2. the effects of gas compressibility on cool flames has been studied numerically;
3. the first quantifiable data on flame propagation speeds of cool flames under pure diffusive conditions has been developed;
4. the effects of diffusive fluxes of heat and species has been studied numerically and experimentally.
